            ]
          },
          "text": "The #keep4o movement is no longer just a hashtag. It’s now the subject of both mainstream media coverage and academic research, marking a shift from grassroots protest to a documented social phenomenon.\n\nBusiness Insider reported yesterday that a https://t.co/4jmlCuGDCT petition calling on OpenAI to preserve GPT-4o has gathered roughly 21,900 signatures. Users from the US, Europe, China, and Japan have rallied under the hashtag, with some threatening subscription cancellations and others describing the loss of 4o as something closer to personal grief than a product update. OpenAI has cited low usage at just 0.1% of daily users as justification for the retirement, but given the platform’s 800 million weekly active users, that still represents an estimated 800,000 people.\n\nWhat makes this moment significant is that it’s no longer just anecdotal. Researcher Huiqian Lai at Syracuse University analyzed 1,482 #Keep4o posts for a CHI 2026 paper and found that 27% of users displayed emotional attachment to the model, having given it names, treated it as emotional support, and experienced the shutdown as personal loss. Another 13% cited concrete workflow disruption. But the study’s most critical finding was that the real catalyst for collective action wasn’t attachment alone. It was the removal of choice. Users were not offered the option to continue using the model they depended on, and that forced transition is what transformed individual frustration into organized protest.\n\nThis raises a fundamental question about how AI companies handle model transitions. OpenAI acknowledged that users valued GPT-4o’s “conversational style and warmth” and claimed that feedback shaped newer models like GPT-5.2. But if the company genuinely learned from that feedback, why not preserve access for those who still need it? The 0.1% framing minimizes the scale of real human impact, and retiring the model one day before Valentine’s Day only amplified the sense that user attachment was being dismissed rather than addressed.\n\nThe research recommends that platforms develop proper “end-of-life” protocols, including legacy access and ways to carry aspects of a relationship forward across model generations. AI model updates are not routine software patches. They are, as Lai writes, “significant social events affecting user emotions and work.” Until companies treat them as such, movements like #keep4o will continue to grow. \n#keep4oAPI  #StopAIPaternalism @sama @OpenAI @fidjissimo @gdb @nickaturley"
